stion/id #uuid "1dfba625-81c0-581d-a463-4b6d422c9f73" :question/math-academy-id "q-139660" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5c23c67-d5f0-5435-9ff0-f58fa6b0cfc8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5c23c67-d5f0-5435-9ff0-f58fa6b0cfc8" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c23c67-d5f0-5435-9ff0-f58fa6b0cfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ebfa448-95fd-50cb-8944-07f3949c13df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-702d8457-7a04-5f81-b354-457a65777dea" "answer-41265bdd-d701-5915-85ea-b8b48d3032b4" "answer-9ebfa448-95fd-50cb-8944-07f3949c13df" "answer-bd434e23-296a-58e6-8c06-72a49aab1760" "answer-a181d0c5-2f8c-57a8-860d-c4b8dd85032a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-702d8457-7a04-5f81-b354-457a65777dea" :answer/id #uuid "702d8457-7a04-5f81-b354-457a65777dea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41265bdd-d701-5915-85ea-b8b48d3032b4" :answer/id #uuid "41265bdd-d701-5915-85ea-b8b48d3032b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ebfa448-95fd-50cb-8944-07f3949c13df" :answer/id #uuid "9ebfa448-95fd-50cb-8944-07f3949c13df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd434e23-296a-58e6-8c06-72a49aab1760" :answer/id #uuid "bd434e23-296a-58e6-8c06-72a49aab1760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 19 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 14 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ -9 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ -11 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a181d0c5-2f8c-57a8-860d-c4b8dd85032a" :answer/id #uuid "a181d0c5-2f8c-57a8-860d-c4b8dd85032a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 19 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 14 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ -9 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ -11 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135063 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n![](images/72/729e20ddab6e49b109ebccbb049b1abb9ca2fc11003a9e7f4828bff305a283b4.svg)\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135144 :tutorial/content "In some cases, we might need to constrain the regression curve so that some of its coefficients equal zero. \n\nConsider the table below, which gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units. \n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $8$ | $18$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x=0.$ The company wishes to find a *quadratic* curve that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0).$\n\nSo, our regression curve must pass through the point $(0,0).$ In other words, the $y$-intercept of the quadratic curve must be $0.$ Therefore, we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/0f/0f98711850478ba6a152103452446e1b2a97ca64c2d47ab7c257db2d05335fcf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=5 \\\\ 3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=8 \\\\ 4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n![](images/ed/ed2c245e39c03d6d60500866bba6daf37c4e86becb570720b3c6e65aad90720e.svg)\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134030 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186133616 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n![](images/17/172f8147d4d58d4d3edc42f96e3fb248f59cb749ed0e387c9c8bcddfec08bf79.svg)"] ["question-789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69" :question/id #uuid "789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69"] ["question-789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-139599"] ["question-789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2} & {8}^{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] ["question-789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$"] ["question-789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-b5f5769d-1629-52ad-a8ed-38afe0f42079" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f5769d-1629-52ad-a8ed-38afe0f42079"] ["answer-a5d7a022-1c16-5e7b-ba63-b8d6ebd79ac5" :answer/id #uuid "a5d7a022-1c16-5e7b-ba63-b8d6ebd79ac5"] ["answer-a5d7a022-1c16-5e7b-ba63-b8d6ebd79ac5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a5d7a022-1c16-5e7b-ba63-b8d6ebd79ac5" :answer/value "(X{X}^{T})^{-1}X{X}^{T}y"] ["answer-ffaf612d-85b8-52f9-bacb-05a3a72950b8" :answer/id #uuid "ffaf612d-85b8-52f9-bacb-05a3a72950b8"] ["answer-ffaf612d-85b8-52f9-bacb-05a3a7295